    "lossyReason": "PLAN.md S.10.2: psdexport emits layer channels in its own fixed order (0,1,2,-1; gray 0,-1) and always emits an alpha channel, because the engine model is RGBA. This fixture was authored with alpha first (-1,0,1,2) or with no alpha channel at all, so every re-exported record's channelIds differ in order and the flattened records gain a -1. The PSD spec does not prescribe channel order and readers key on the ID, so this is a spelling difference rather than lost data: the set of IDs is otherwise unchanged, the -2 user mask channel included. psd-tools 1.19.0 and ImageMagick 6.9.12-98 both read the re-export correctly. PLAN.md S.10.3: psdRecords[6].sectionType - the open/closed folder flag (lsct 1 vs 2) is parsed correctly but lost at import, because the engine model has nowhere to store a group's expanded state, so psdexport writes every group as LayerSectionOpenFolder and the closed folder re-opens as 1. There is no model-scope path that can carry this, which is why the record leg is the only one that sees it. PLAN.md S.10.3: psdRecords[N].psdBlendKey on the hidden `</Layer group>` bounding dividers (lsct 3) - psdexport's newGroupEndRecord hardcodes the divider's blend key to `pass`, while this fixture's writer emitted `norm`. Which one Photoshop writes is NOT established: no Photoshop-authored file is available to check against, and the spec does not prescribe it. The field is inert - readers identify the record by lsct 3, and both psd-tools 1.19.0 and ImageMagick 6.9.12-98 read the re-export correctly - so it is recorded rather than changed on a guess. Note that psdexport is internally inconsistent here: newGroupRecord writes `pass` only for a non-isolated group, but the divider gets `pass` unconditionally. Resolve against a real Photoshop file.",
